## Node Discovery

{{ provisioner_name }} can do dynamic discovery, but here we will do node
discovery by YAML file. The YAML file can be imported by the discover command
creating a way for adding nodes that do not have redfish capabilities, or when
you just want to provide a list of nodes.

Create the `nodes.yaml` file the static discovery will use to populate SMD.
Also add the compute nodes to `/etc/hosts` for SSH access, with both the short
and the fully qualified name, `<name> <name>.${dns_domain}`, as xCAT's
`makehosts` and Confluent's `confluent2hosts` write them; tools such as the test
suite's `koomie_cf` find hosts only by a dotted name.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Discovering Nodes -->
```bash
mkdir -p /opt/ohpc/admin/nodes
nf=/opt/ohpc/admin/nodes/nodes.yaml

## Add BMCs
echo "bmcs:" > $nf
for((i=0; i < $num_computes; i++)); do
	nid=$((i+1))
	printf -v mac '%02x:%02x' "$(((nid >> 8) & 0xff))" "$((nid & 0xff))"
	cat >> $nf << EOF
- xname: x1000c0s0b${nid}
  ip: ${c_bmc[$i]}
  mac: 02:00:00:00:${mac}
EOF
done

## Add nodes
echo "nodes:" >> $nf
for((i=0; i < $num_computes; i++)); do
	nid=$((i+1))
	cat >> $nf << EOF
- name: ${c_name[$i]}
  nid: ${nid}
  xname: x1000c0s0b${nid}n0
  bmc: x1000c0s0b${nid}
  groups:
  - compute
  interfaces:
  - mac_addr: ${c_mac[$i]}
    ip_addrs:
    - name: internal
      ip_addr: ${c_ip[$i]}
EOF
done

## Add nodes to /etc/hosts
for((i=0; i < $num_computes; i++)); do
	echo "${c_ip[$i]} ${c_name[$i]} ${c_name[$i]}.${dns_domain:-local}" >> /etc/hosts
done
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

If IPoIB is configured on the compute nodes, add each one to its IPoIB group.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Add the computes to their IPoIB groups -->
<!-- ohpc_if_set enable_ipoib -->
```bash
yq -i '.nodes[] |= (.groups += ["ipoib-" + .name])' \
	/opt/ohpc/admin/nodes/nodes.yaml
```
<!-- ohpc_fi -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Now use the generated file to populate the SMD database.

With {{ provisioner_name }} 0.2.0, `ochami discover static` reports a `409
Conflict` for each BMC ("an existing component ethernet interface that has the
same MAC address") and exits non-zero, even on an empty SMD, although every
record is written. So check what SMD holds rather than the exit status.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Passing ochami the nodes -->
```bash
ochami discover static \
	-f yaml -d @/opt/ohpc/admin/nodes/nodes.yaml \
	|| echo "ochami discover static reported errors, checking SMD"
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

`ochami discover static` populates SMD components, EthernetInterfaces (under the correct
node xname), and group membership from the `nodes.yaml` file in a single step.
Confirm that each compute node's interface and each BMC's endpoint are there:

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Verify the nodes are in SMD -->
```bash
for((i=0; i < $num_computes; i++)); do
	ochami -k smd iface get \
		| jq -e --arg m "${c_mac[$i]}" 'any(.[]; .MACAddress == $m)' >/dev/null \
		|| { echo "SMD: FAILED, no interface for ${c_name[$i]}"; exit 1; }
	ochami -k smd rfe get \
		| jq -e --arg x "x1000c0s0b$((i+1))" 'any(.RedfishEndpoints[]; .ID == $x)' \
		>/dev/null \
		|| { echo "SMD: FAILED, no BMC endpoint for ${c_name[$i]}"; exit 1; }
done
echo "SMD: OK"
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->
